n such  as your google login  and asks whether you grant the app  permissions to access your data  in one-way tls which is another means of  securing  the client identifies the server with  the certificate presented by that server  with a two-way tls connection the server  similarly identifies the client  tls certificates for clients can be  difficult to manage and they generally  aren't used with open api programs  though they are certainly used for some  partners that may access privileged apis  often in conjunction with other  authentication mechanisms for example  third-party payment processors may be  using a bank's actual payment apis  often have to have their servers present  client certificates as part of their  access  like i already mentioned api key is  really used for its simplicity we highly  recommend oauth  there are different grant types that can  be utilized based on the level of trust  and other requirements  a simple client credentials grant type  where a system passes a client id and a  secret might work well  for a trusted internal system a  third-party app that allows access to  individual data on the other hand would  need to use an authorization code flow  like i previously described where the  consumer authorizes the access  finally i'll mention that tls certs used  for two-way tls  make a lot of sense when you're using a  service mesh that can manage all of that  for you  one of the things in common with all of  these methods is that they identify the  calling client  oauth tokens however can also identify  not just the client but the end user as  well  so dino what situations do you see  customers using these different  mechanisms to secure their api access  well the api key is a good basic  building block  it's appropriate for use with an open  api where you want to track who's  calling the api  and also it can be used as a key  identifier  when doing signing or encryption i'll  talk  more about that later oauth is pretty  common as you s