n validate the  recaptcha token  before granting service and recaptcha  will issue an opinion about that client  is it a robot or is it not using  recapture this way  recaptcha acts as a gatekeeper or  bouncer for your front door  it does mean introducing a new external  dependency  into your system but you may want to  accept that to get the additional level  of security  perfect thank you  so when do you use what api access token  pretty much is always going to be in  place there are some exceptions for  truly public info  but even then you may want to provide  some level of access control  signatures can be useful when you need  to verify the integrity of the payload  or can even be used for distributed  access like the sign url example  encryption is useful when a message may  be passed around between multiple  systems in an asynchronous manner  or for the most sensitive data you want  to ensure that even if the wires are  compromised the content is still secure  this can also be used to ensure that  messages are secure even when written to  disk or if they're inadvertently written  to logs  there are multiple areas where  anomalated bot detection might make  sense  but we recommend that this is at least  used to protect your login and token api  ideally you want to prevent the bad  actors from ever getting a token in the  first place  with that let me turn it over to dino  who walk us through how we might be able  to implement some of this inside of  apigee  information driven companies will have  tens or hundreds of services that they  use to run their business  some of them custom built maybe in java  golang c sharp  python maybe running in a vm or in  kubernetes some of these services  might be third party sas there are many  different  implementation options apogee can help  manage all of them  to do that we rely on an apogee proxy  that intercedes between clients of the  service  and the service this allows us to  standardize  on the access message and con